LEAP Program Portfolio Report

Executive Summary

This report analyzes baseline clinical and biochemical factors associated with quantitative diabetes disease 
progression over a one-year follow-up period in a sample of N=442 patients. 
Using exploratory data analysis, pairwise Pearson correlation coefficients, 
multiple linear regression, and diagnostic goodness-of-fit evaluations, we assess the primary predictive indicators of the disease. 
Body Mass Index (bmi), Blood Pressure (bp), and Serum Triglycerides (s5) emerge as the most impactful baseline prognostic factors.
Crucial methodological parameters are also examined, including the distinction between statistical and clinical significance, 
addressing severe multicollinearity among laboratory serum markers,
and acknowledging the limitations of drawing causal inferences from observational data.

Question 1: Understand and Prepare the Data

The dataset contains 442 patient observations with 10 initial independent variables and 1 continuous outcome variable measuring
quantitative disease progression one year post-baseline (25≤Y≤346). Demographic predictors include age (age) and sex (sex), 
which is numerically coded (1=Male, 2=Female). Physical measurements consist of Body Mass Index (bmi, in kg/m 
2) and mean arterial blood pressure (bp, in mm Hg). Additionally, six serum biochemical measurements are included: 
total serum cholesterol (s1), low-density lipoproteins / LDL (s2), high-density lipoproteins / HDL (s3),
total cholesterol to HDL ratio (s4),log serum triglycerides (s5), and blood glucose level (s6).   
During data integrity checks, 0 missing values (NA) and 0 duplicate rows were confirmed across all 442 observations. 
The sex variable was converted from numeric codes (1,2) into a categorical factor with levels Male and Female, 
ensuring R treats it as a qualitative variable with dummy coding rather than a continuous numeric scale. 
From an ethical and clinical perspective, responsible health data handling is essential: statistical models 
serve as correlation-seeking tools and must not be misinterpreted as automated diagnostic systems or definitive proof of causality 
without expert medical evaluation.

Question 2: Explore the Data

Descriptive statistical analysis captures the central tendency and dispersion of key variables. 
The outcome variable (progression) exhibits a mean of 152.14, a standard deviation of σ=77.09, a median of 140.50, 
an interquartile range of IQR=102.00, and a range spanning 25.00 to 346.00. Body Mass Index (bmi) presents a mean of 26.38 kg/m 
2(σ=4.42, median 25.75, IQR=5.38, minimum 18.00, maximum 42.20), 
representing a broad clinical cross-section from underweight to severely obese patients. 
Blood pressure (bp) records a mean of 94.64 mm Hg (σ=13.83, median 92.00, IQR=19.00, minimum 62.00, maximum 133.00), 
while patient age ranges from 19.00 to 79.00 years with a mean of 48.52 years (σ=13.11).   
Morphologically, the distribution of disease progression (progression) displays mild right skewness, 
whereas bmi follows an approximately normal distribution with slight right-skewing. 
Visual inspection via boxplots confirms the absence of extreme mathematical outliers requiring trimming or winsorization, 
allowing for the retention of the full sample in subsequent modeling.   

Question 3: Investigate Relationships

Bivariate Pearson correlation coefficients (r) reveal the strongest linear associations with disease progression:   
bmi vs. progression: r=0.586 (Strong positive linear association)   
s5 (Triglycerides) vs. progression: r=0.566 (Strong positive linear association)   
bp vs. progression: r=0.441 (Moderate positive linear association)   
s4 vs. progression: r=0.430 (Moderate positive association)   
s3 (HDL) vs. progression: r=−0.395 (Moderate negative association)   
age vs. progression: r=0.188 (Weak positive association)   
Simultaneously, severe collinearity is present among laboratory serum metrics: total cholesterol (s1) and LDL (s2) 
share a correlation coefficient of r=0.897, while HDL (s3) and ratio s4 display r=−0.738. 
Including these variables concurrently in a multiple regression model creates a risk of inflated standard errors and 
parameter estimate instability.   

Question 4: Build and Interpret Regression Models

The baseline simple linear regression model using Body Mass Index as the single predictor is formulated as:   
progression=−117.08+10.21⋅(bmi)+ϵ
The intercept ( 
β=−117.08,p<0.001) lacks practical clinical meaning as it corresponds to a non-physiological BMI of zero. The slope ( 
β=10.21,p<0.001) indicates that for every 1 kg/m increase in BMI, expected 1-year disease progression increases by 10.21 units. 
The coefficient of determination (R =0.344) shows that BMI alone explains 34.4% of total variance in disease progression.   
Next, a parsimonious multiple regression model was built using the three most impactful clinical indicators (bmi, bp, s5):   
progression=−155.22+7.87⋅(bmi)+0.98⋅(bp)+37.52⋅(s5)+ϵ
Under the ceteris paribus assumption (holding all other predictors constant):   
Each 1 kg/m increase in BMI increases expected progression by 7.87 units (p<0.001).   
Each 1 mm Hg increase in blood pressure increases expected progression by 0.98 units (p<0.001).   
Each 1 unit increase in log triglycerides increases expected progression by 37.52 units (p<0.001).   
Adjusted R reaches 0.477 (F=134.7,p<0.001), demonstrating that the combined model explains 47.7% of variance, 
outperforming the simple model. 

Question 5: Evaluate the Analysis

Evaluating multicollinearity using Variance Inflation Factors (VIF) in the full 10-variable model reveals extreme 
inflation among serum markers:
s1 (VIF=59.20), s2 (VIF=39.19), s3 (VIF=15.40), and s5 (VIF=10.08). 
In contrast, the parsimonious 3-variable model (bmi, bp, s5) keeps VIF values below 1.53 across all predictors, 
ensuring stable coefficient estimation.   
Diagnostic residual plots (Residuals vs. Fitted) for the model confirm assumptions of linearity and homoscedasticity, 
showing random point dispersion around zero. The Normal Q-Q plot verifies residual normality. 
95% confidence intervals for the model coefficients are:   
bmi: [6.45,9.29]   
bp: [0.64,1.32]   
s5: [28.02,47.01]   
Because none of these intervals cross zero, we confirm strong statistical evidence of positive associations.   

Question 6: Draw Appropriate Conclusions

The core findings demonstrate that baseline BMI, Blood Pressure, and Serum Triglycerides are the single most 
important clinical indicators of 1-year diabetes progression. 
A concise 3-variable model efficiently captures 47.7% of disease progression variance while maintaining numerical stability and 
avoiding multicollinearity.   
However, key methodological limitations apply. The dataset is observational; regression coefficients imply correlation rather than 
causation. 
Mechanically reducing a patient's BMI does not guarantee a directly proportional decrease in 
disease progression without evidence from prospective clinical trials.
Furthermore, omitted variable bias remains possible, as unmeasured behavioral or biological factors (such as dietary habits, 
physical exercise, medication adherence, and genetic markers) were absent from the dataset.
